INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE, LONDON - NIGHT (1912)

Brass keys. Glass cases. A gas lamp. A wall of pigeonholes. The tick of a dozen sounders. Rain on the glass.

MR. PERCIVAL ASHDOWN, 50s, stiff collar, spectacles, sorts slips. MISS ROSALIND TREVETHAN, 20s, clerk, hair pinned up under a cap, taps the key. LORD ALISTAIR CROYDE, 50s, in a fur-collared coat, waits at the counter, drumming gloved fingers. And a woman, HARRIET LUCAS, 30s, in a plain brown coat, stands at the far end of the counter holding an unopened envelope.

Ashdown clears his throat.

ASHDOWN
(to the room)
Ladies and gentlemen. We are experiencing delay. The Atlantic cable is busy.

CROYDE
(to Ashdown)
How busy?

ASHDOWN
Very.

CROYDE
Is it the Titanic?

Silence. Rosalind's hand stills on the key.

ASHDOWN
It is, my lord.

Harriet puts the envelope down.

HARRIET
(under her breath)
My brother was a stoker.

ROSALIND
(to Harriet)
Miss, I am so sorry.

The sounder clatters. Rosalind leans in, writes, tears the slip.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Message. Carpathia has picked up survivors.

A breath goes through the office. Croyde takes off his hat. Ashdown snatches the slip and reads it.

ASHDOWN
(to Rosalind)
Is there a list?

ROSALIND
Not yet.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(over the sounder, faint, relayed)
Names to follow. Standby, London.

Harriet takes a step forward, then stops.

HARRIET
(to herself)
Please, Tom. Please.

CROYDE
(to Ashdown)
Mr. Ashdown, send a message to my wife. Tell her I have an interest.

ASHDOWN
My lord, there are priorities.

CROYDE
I am aware of that, Ashdown.

Rosalind keys again. The sounder answers.

ROSALIND
(reading)
Names. First batch.

The whole room leans in. Rosalind begins to read aloud, each name a stone dropped into a well.
